@page "/guides/telemetry"
@using Trailblazor.Docs.Examples

<PageTitle>Telemetry · Trailblazor</PageTitle>

<TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Headline" As="TextElement.H1">Telemetry</TbText>
<TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Subtitle" class="docs-lead">
    Trailblazor's components count what people see and use, so you can tell which features matter and which nobody
    touches, in the dashboard you already have. Label a control with <code>TelemetryName</code>, listen to the
    <code>Trailblazor</code> meter, and the counts arrive with the rest of your app's telemetry. Nothing is recorded
    until you listen, and nothing leaves your app unless you send it.
</TbText>

<TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Title" As="TextElement.H2" class="docs-section">Try it</TbText>
<TbText>
    These buttons carry a <code>TelemetryName</code>, and this page listens to the <code>Trailblazor</code> meter in
    your browser, as your app's OpenTelemetry setup would. Each button counts as shown when it renders, and as used
    when you click it. The disabled one is shown but can't be used, which is exactly the kind of gap the counts reveal.
</TbText>
<TelemetryDemo />

<TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Title" As="TextElement.H2" class="docs-section">What it's for</TbText>
<ul class="docs-notes">
    <li>
        <strong>Feature adoption, with the zeros.</strong> Analytics tools count the clicks they see, so a feature
        nobody uses simply never appears. Trailblazor counts a labeled control as shown when it renders, so a feature
        that is shown but never used stands out, and you can see how often each one is reached at all.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Reports per customer.</strong> Add your own tags, such as the customer or plan a user belongs to, and
        every count carries them. A team that ships one app to many customers can show each of them what their people
        use.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>In your own pipeline.</strong> The counts go through .NET's standard metrics, so OpenTelemetry sends
        them wherever your app already sends its telemetry: the Aspire dashboard, Prometheus and Grafana, Azure Monitor,
        or any OpenTelemetry backend. There is no Trailblazor service and no account.
    </li>
</ul>

<TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Title" As="TextElement.H2" class="docs-section">Set it up</TbText>
<TbText>
    <code>AddTrailblazor</code> already registers the meter. Listen to it in your OpenTelemetry setup, with the
    exporter for your backend:
</TbText>
<CodeBlock Label="Program.cs" Code="@Setup" />
<TbText>Then label the controls you want counted:</TbText>
<CodeBlock Label="Labeled buttons" Code="@Labels" />

<TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Title" As="TextElement.H2" class="docs-section">Choosing names</TbText>
<ul class="docs-notes">
    <li>
        A <code>TelemetryName</code> is a label you put on a control so the app can count how often people see it and
        use it. It names what the control does for the user, never the user.
    </li>
    <li>
        Controls that share a name count together. Give the toolbar's Export button and the dialog's Export button the
        same <code>"export-csv"</code> to count the feature, or different names to compare the two ways in.
    </li>
    <li>
        A control without a name isn't counted, so leave it off buttons such as OK and Cancel that aren't worth
        measuring.
    </li>
    <li>
        Keep names stable, short and few: dashboards group by them. A name made from data, such as a record's id,
        would split the counts and could leak what users work on. It isn't a credential, and it never hides or gates a
        control.
    </li>
    <li>
        Buttons, icon buttons and link buttons take it today. Menu items, tabs, toggles, chips, selects, data grids
        and tree views follow; see the <a href="roadmap">roadmap</a>.
    </li>
</ul>

<TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Title" As="TextElement.H2" class="docs-section">What's recorded</TbText>
<TbText>
    Every instrument of the <code>@TelemetryCatalog.MeterName</code> meter, with its tags:
    <code>trailblazor.telemetry_name</code> is the control's <code>TelemetryName</code> and
    <code>trailblazor.component</code> its type, such as <code>TbButton</code>. Each also carries any tags your app
    adds.
</TbText>
<div class="docs-api" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="The Trailblazor meter's instruments">
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th scope="col">Instrument</th>
                <th scope="col">Type</th>
                <th scope="col">Unit</th>
                <th scope="col">Tags</th>
                <th scope="col">Recorded when</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            @foreach (var instrument in TelemetryCatalog.Instruments)
            {
                <tr>
                    <th scope="row"><code>@instrument.Name</code></th>
                    <td>@instrument.Kind</td>
                    <td><code>@instrument.Unit</code></td>
                    <td>
                        @foreach (var tag in instrument.Tags)
                        {
                            <code>@tag</code>@(" ")
                        }
                    </td>
                    <td>@instrument.When</td>
                </tr>
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>
<TbText>
    Each component's page lists what that component reports, under Telemetry. Counts start with the first interactive
    render, so a statically rendered page reports nothing, and a prerendered one counts once, not twice.
</TbText>

<TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Title" As="TextElement.H2" class="docs-section">What's never recorded</TbText>
<ul class="docs-notes">
    <li>
        Values, text people type, dialog and page titles, labels' text and anything about the user. Only the names you
        give and the components' types, by design and not configurable.
    </li>
    <li>
        Nothing is recorded until something listens to the meter, and nothing leaves the app unless the app exports
        it. Trailblazor never sends anything anywhere itself.
    </li>
    <li>
        Your own tags are yours to keep clean: they go on every count, so never put a user's name, email or id in
        them.
    </li>
    <li>
        Sending telemetry from the browser, as a WebAssembly app does, can need the user's consent under EU rules even
        when no personal data is in it. Ask your own privacy advisers; the app decides when to export.
    </li>
</ul>

<TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Title" As="TextElement.H2" class="docs-section">Your own tags</TbText>
<TbText>
    Register an <code>ITelemetryTags</code>, scoped when the tags depend on who is signed in, and its tags go on every
    count. Keep each tag to a small set of values, such as a customer or a plan, since every value is a series in the
    dashboard.
</TbText>
<CodeBlock Label="Tags for the user's customer" Code="@Tags" />

<TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Title" As="TextElement.H2" class="docs-section">Render modes</TbText>
<ul class="docs-notes">
    <li>
        <strong>Interactive Server:</strong> the components run on the server, so the server's OpenTelemetry setup
        exports the counts with nothing more to do.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>WebAssembly:</strong> the components run in the browser. Turn on <code>MetricsSupport</code> in the
        client project, which .NET 10 leaves off for WebAssembly, use OpenTelemetry .NET 1.15.2 or later, which can
        export from the browser's single thread, and send over HTTP with protobuf to an endpoint on your own server
        that forwards to your collector. Your server adds any keys, so none reach the browser and no CORS setup is
        needed. WebAssembly runs no hosted services, so resolve OpenTelemetry's <code>MeterProvider</code> before
        running the app, or nothing starts listening. The Aspire dashboard's guide to Blazor WebAssembly telemetry
        shows the relay.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Auto:</strong> set up both: the server's export for its renders, and the client's for after the
        WebAssembly runtime takes over.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Static rendering:</strong> nothing is counted, since nothing runs after the page is sent.</li>
</ul>
<CodeBlock Label="The WebAssembly client" Code="@WebAssembly" />

<TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Title" As="TextElement.H2" class="docs-section">Reading the counts</TbText>
<TbText>
    In the Aspire dashboard, open Metrics, choose the app and the <code>Trailblazor</code> meter, then
    <code>trailblazor.control.used</code>, and split it by <code>trailblazor.telemetry_name</code>. In Prometheus the
    OpenTelemetry exporter names the counters <code>trailblazor_control_shown_total</code> and
    <code>trailblazor_control_used_total</code>, and the tag <code>trailblazor_telemetry_name</code>. Features shown in
    the last month but never used:
</TbText>
<CodeBlock Label="Shown but never used, in PromQL" Code="@NeverUsed" />

<TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Title" As="TextElement.H2" class="docs-section">Testing</TbText>
<TbText>
    Check what a page reports in bUnit with <code>MetricCollector&lt;T&gt;</code> from
    <code>Microsoft.Extensions.Diagnostics.Testing</code>. Pass it the test's <code>IMeterFactory</code>, so it hears
    only this test's components, even with other tests running alongside.
</TbText>
<CodeBlock Label="A bUnit test" Code="@Test" />

<TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Title" As="TextElement.H2" class="docs-section">Cost and turning it off</TbText>
<TbText>
    When nothing listens, a count is one check, well under a nanosecond. When something does, a count costs tens of
    nanoseconds, far less than a render. An app that listens to every meter but doesn't want its UI counted turns the
    components off:
</TbText>
<CodeBlock Label="Turning the counts off" Code="@Off" />

<TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Title" As="TextElement.H2" class="docs-section">API</TbText>
<ApiTable Type="typeof(TelemetrySettings)" />
<ApiTable Type="typeof(ITelemetryTags)" />

@code {
    private const string Setup = """
        builder.Services.AddTrailblazor();
        builder.Services.AddOpenTelemetry()
            .WithMetrics(metrics => metrics
                .AddMeter("Trailblazor")
                .AddOtlpExporter());
        """;

    private const string Labels = """
        <TbButton TelemetryName="export-csv" OnClick="ExportAsync">Export</TbButton>
        <TbIconButton TelemetryName="share-link" Icon="MaterialSymbols.Outlined.Share" AriaLabel="Share"
                      OnClick="Share" />
        <TbButton TelemetryName="trail-guide" Href="guides/trails" Variant="Variant.Text">Trail guide</TbButton>
        """;

    private const string Tags = """
        public sealed class CustomerTags(CurrentCustomer customer) : ITelemetryTags
        {
            public IEnumerable<KeyValuePair<string, object?>> Tags => [new("app.customer", customer.Slug)];
        }

        builder.Services.AddScoped<ITelemetryTags, CustomerTags>();
        """;

    private const string WebAssembly = """
        <!-- Client.csproj -->
        <PropertyGroup>
          <MetricsSupport>true</MetricsSupport>
        </PropertyGroup>

        // Client Program.cs: send to the app's own server, which forwards to the collector.
        builder.Services.AddOpenTelemetry()
            .WithMetrics(metrics => metrics
                .AddMeter("Trailblazor")
                .AddOtlpExporter(exporter =>
                {
                    exporter.Protocol = OtlpExportProtocol.HttpProtobuf;
                    exporter.Endpoint = new Uri(new Uri(builder.HostEnvironment.BaseAddress), "_otlp/v1/metrics");
                }));

        var host = builder.Build();
        // WebAssembly runs no hosted services, so OpenTelemetry starts listening once its provider is resolved.
        host.Services.GetRequiredService<MeterProvider>();
        await host.RunAsync();
        """;

    private const string NeverUsed = """
        sum by (trailblazor_telemetry_name) (increase(trailblazor_control_shown_total[30d]))
          unless
        sum by (trailblazor_telemetry_name) (increase(trailblazor_control_used_total[30d]) > 0)
        """;

    private const string Test = """
        [Fact]
        public void Export_ShouldBeCounted_WhenClicked()
        {
            this.AddTrailblazor();
            SetRendererInfo(new RendererInfo("Server", isInteractive: true));
            using var used = new MetricCollector<long>(Services.GetRequiredService<IMeterFactory>(),
                "Trailblazor", "trailblazor.control.used");
            var page = Render<ReportsPage>();

            page.Find("button").Click();

            var count = Assert.Single(used.GetMeasurementSnapshot());
            Assert.Equal("export-csv", count.Tags["trailblazor.telemetry_name"]);
        }
        """;

    private const string Off = """
        builder.Services.AddTrailblazor(options => options.Telemetry.Enabled = false);
        """;
}
